const intro={"n":33,"title":"The Final Canto: New Names, Gaucho Rights and the Poet's Farewell","nums":[1175,1176,1182,1188,1193],"summary":"The four companions go in different directions and agree upon a secret promise whose details the narrator explicitly refuses to reveal. They also agree to change their names. The closing voice then addresses the audience, turns to the gaucho's social exclusion, and argues that gauchos should have homes, schools, churches and rights. It explicitly announces thirty-three cantos in this second part, recalls the suffering shared with compatriots and closes by presenting the song as intended for everyone's benefit. The secret is never disclosed in the canto.","people":"Martín Fierro, his two sons and Cruz's son Picardía as the four who separate; the closing poetic narrator addressing readers and listeners.","themes":"Unrevealed promises and changing identities; separation and new lives; social rights and educational access; authorial voice and literary closure; collective memory.","words":"cuatro vientos (the four winds, different directions); gaucho (rural horseman and social figure); escuela (school); derechos (rights); paisanos (countrymen); treinta y tres cantos (thirty-three cantos of the sequel).","questions":["How many companions separate at the opening, and what remains secret?","What do they agree to change, and why must the motive not be presented as certain?","Which specific institutions and protections does the closing voice demand for gauchos?","How does stanza 1188 identify the number of cantos in the second part?","What does the closing stanza say about the intended purpose of the poem?"],"analysis":"The text does not explain the promise; inventing its contents would corrupt the reading. Changing names is stated, while the narrator speculates generally about concealment. Stanza 1182 makes a concrete social demand for a home, school, church and rights. The farewell shifts from the four characters' actions into the poetic speaker's appeal to collective memory, not a report of later adventures.","related":"/en/students/vuelta-canto-32/","relatedTitle":"Fierro's advice before the final separation"};
